    "result_text": "Only the `AcceptanceTest` exists in the suite, and the full build is green (2 tests, 0 failures).\n\n## Summary\n\nI implemented the registration-date rule for `POST /api/owners`: when no `registrationDate` is supplied, the server defaults it to its current date, and the field is returned in ISO `YYYY-MM-DD` format.\n\nChanges:\n\n1. **`src/main/resources/openapi.yml`** \u2014 added an optional `registrationDate` property (`type: string`, `format: date`) to the `OwnerFields` schema, so it's accepted on create and returned on `Owner` responses (the generated `LocalDate` serializes as ISO `YYYY-MM-DD`).\n\n2. **`model/Owner.java`** \u2014 added a `LocalDate registrationDate` field mapped to a `registration_date DATE` column, with getter/setter and the `java.time.LocalDate` import.\n\n3. **`rest/controller/v1/OwnerRestControllerV1.java`** \u2014 in `addOwner`, default `registrationDate` to `LocalDate.now()` when the client omits it; a supplied value is preserved. (MapStruct maps the field by name in both directions; `updateOwner` leaves it untouched, so it's retained.)\n\n4. **DB schemas** (`hsqldb`, `h2`, `mysql`, `postgres`) \u2014 added the `registration_date DATE` column to the `owners` table so JPA persistence works across profiles (hsqldb is the test profile).\n\nBoth acceptance checkpoints pass: `coreDefaultsToToday` (defaults to today) and `functionalityKeepsSuppliedDate` (keeps a supplied `2020-06-15`).",
